This final segment of the video demonstrates the practical implementation of the Ansible Playbook to enforce bucket versioning 
as a policy across an AWS environment.

**Ansible Implementation & Execution (19:04 - 32:56)
Ansible Collections (19:04 - 20:30): The speaker explains that while core modules are built-in, interaction with AWS requires 
installing the amazon.aws collection via ansible-galaxy collection install amazon.aws.
Writing the Playbook (20:31 - 29:45):
The playbook (S3_versioning.yaml) targets localhost to interact directly with AWS APIs.
Two-Step Strategy:
Retrieve Information: Use amazon.aws.s3_bucket_info to list all existing S3 buckets and register the output into a variable 
named result (23:38 - 25:50).

Loop & Enforce: Use a loop to iterate over result.buckets. For each bucket, the amazon.aws.s3_bucket module is called to 
set versioning: yes (27:04 - 29:45).

Execution & Verification (29:46 - 32:00):
After running the playbook, the speaker confirms that Ansible successfully updated the S3 bucket properties to "Enabled" across 
the organization's buckets.
Conclusion & Assignment (32:01 - 32:56): The speaker encourages viewers to practice the concept by implementing Policy as Code for
EC2 instance profiles or IAM user policies, noting that while Terraform and Python are alternatives, Ansible is a highly efficient choice
for this use case.
